{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Operator decisions round 2 (2026-07-29):\n\nAPPROVED / IN-SCOPE: R1 verification-presence floor (blocking). Mid-review cancellation (own-material predicate design). edit --review + claim --review flags. genai-prices — as OPTIONAL add-in with graceful fallback when not installed (cost column reads unavailable, never an error). T5c rewrite to field-consistent code grounding. Coaching moves into plan guidance. Docs suppression fix — valid doc findings being suppressed is a bug; do not block on nits, but doc quality meaningfully impacts codebase accuracy and future-agent success (impact-aware suppression direction). DET triggers for zero-finding criteria — pending counter-example/false-negative audit (in progress). T3 + T10 blocking enablement. T5c threshold @0.90.\n\nREJECTED / DESCOPED: Split-brain remediation — narrow edge case given push-on-ticket-change policy; high risk of unintended regression. T11 one-per-plan cap — rejected; keep only the interpretation-change constraint, and it MUST be portable across diverse stacks (no rebar-specific anti-FP language).\n\nPENDING DATA (agents running): R2 fixed-lexicon regex backtested against history now (no advisory waiting period if data supports blocking). DET trigger evasion analysis (bias toward firing; silent never-evaluate is the failure mode). G7 threshold FP verification before enabling contradicts_reality blocking. T5c rubric-tightening necessity check vs existing Pass-2 validity/impact filtering.\n\nT11 PORTABLE CONSTRAINT DRAFT (stack-agnostic): raise post-rollback-state findings ONLY when the plan (a) changes how existing persisted records are interpreted, (b) rewrites/migrates records in place, (c) removes or narrows a read path, or (d) performs a one-way transformation with no back-out. Do NOT raise for purely additive, backward-compatible persistence changes that existing readers ignore (new event/record types, optional fields, new tables/columns with defaults) — in append-only or backward-compatible systems, residual data after rollback is the norm, not a risk. No store-technology named; applies equally to SQL migrations, event stores, document DBs, config formats."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785383405063811001,"uuid":"71c9eb16-486c-4e55-8f70-12d74d26426f"}